    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** and **Kyle Tucker** serving as the clear offensive anchors. **Gunnar Henderson** (81.88) benefits from a prime matchup against Peralta, whose xERA of 4.35 and slow delivery offer a high-upside environment for both extra-base hits and stolen bases. Similarly, **Kyle Tucker** (77.36) remains a set-and-forget play against Chandler, as the 19% boost from the opposing SP\u2019s skill deficiency provides a massive lift to his efficiency baseline. **Andy Pages** (71.58) also slots into the \"Strong\" category, leveraging the same favorable matchup against Chandler and a prime #3 batting order position to maximize his counting stat potential.\n\nRegarding marginal starts, **Coby Mayo** (56.02) provides the most utility despite a sub-60 score, while **Nasim Nunez** (49.90) and **Heliot Ramos** (49.84) are buoyed by situational factors\u2014specifically Nunez\u2019s ability to capitalize on Gusto\u2019s slow delivery for stolen base opportunities. The most difficult bench decision involved **Dominic Smith**, who finished at 38.13; despite a platoon edge against Misiorowski, the matchup against an elite arm (2.09 xERA) pushed him below our 40.0 floor, prioritizing game cap preservation over a marginal attempt at production. \n\nRisks remain focused on the top of the lineup; **Drake Baldwin** (45.23) is catching a difficult assignment against Misiorowski, and while his 0.380 xwOBA keeps him viable, his output is capped by the SP\u2019s skill. We are closely monitoring the status of our sidelined core, specifically **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** and **Jordan Westburg**, whose absence continues to compress our depth. While **Luis Robert Jr.** (42.17) rounds out the outfield, his score is severely dampened by a 15% platoon disadvantage against Burke, making him a candidate for mid-game substitution if the game script shifts against the Waves.",
